<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Andrew III of Hungary</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1265–1301</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Andrew III was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> from 1290 and the last male member of the Árpád dynasty. His reign was marked by the growing power of barons such as Matthew Csák, who built regional domains as royal authority weakened. His death in 1301 led to a contested succession, won by Charles I of the Angevin house.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/andrew-iii-of-hungary.jpg" alt="Andrew III of Hungary" width="320" height="383" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: János Thuróczy · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Andrew_III_(Chronica_Hungarorum).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
